## E10 ACL: a cache-application user, and what key patterns do and do not restrict
$ redis-cli -p 7206 ACL SETUSER cacheapp on >test-only-secret ~app:* +@read +@write +@connection -@dangerous
OK
$ redis-cli -p 7206 ACL GETUSER cacheapp
flags
on
sanitize-payload
passwords
64af848afc4e507188b584e1bf9bdece3fe17bce49e77a86a6f97004fd5face5
commands
-@all +@read +@write +@connection -@dangerous
keys
~app:*
channels

selectors

AUTH cacheapp test-only-secret -> +OK
SET app:1 v -> +OK
GET app:1 -> $1
GET other:1 -> -NOPERM No permissions to access a key
SET other:1 v -> -NOPERM No permissions to access a key
KEYS * -> -NOPERM User cacheapp has no permissions to run the 'keys' command
FLUSHALL -> -NOPERM User cacheapp has no permissions to run the 'flushall' command
CONFIG GET maxmemory -> -NOPERM User cacheapp has no permissions to run the 'config|get' command
INFO memory -> -NOPERM User cacheapp has no permissions to run the 'info' command
DEL app:1 -> :1
SCAN 0 -> *2
